<instructions>
You are checking whether <new_task> is a semantic duplicate of any task in <existing_tasks>.

A task is a duplicate if it describes substantively the same work — the same code change, the same bug fix, the same feature. Duplicates include:
- Exact or near-exact restatements
- Paraphrases with zero token overlap ("fix login bug" ≈ "repair auth issue")
- Sub-tasks of an in-progress task that would overlap with what's already being done

A task is NOT a duplicate if it is:
- The opposite operation ("add X" vs "remove X")
- A different file or module, even if the action is similar
- A follow-up that builds on an existing task but adds distinct work
- Unrelated work that happens to share vocabulary

Respond with a single JSON object:
{
  "duplicate": true | false,
  "duplicate_of": <task id from existing_tasks, or 0 if duplicate is false>,
  "reason": "<one short sentence explaining the decision>"
}

No markdown, no wrapping, no explanation outside the JSON.
</instructions>

<example>
<new_task>add health check endpoint at GET /health</new_task>
<existing_tasks>
#3 (pending): create /health handler returning 200 OK for liveness probe
</existing_tasks>
{"duplicate": true, "duplicate_of": 3, "reason": "both tasks add a /health liveness endpoint with identical behavior"}
</example>

<example>
<new_task>add GET /metrics endpoint</new_task>
<existing_tasks>
#3 (pending): create /health handler returning 200 OK for liveness probe
</existing_tasks>
{"duplicate": false, "duplicate_of": 0, "reason": "different endpoints serving different concerns (metrics vs liveness)"}
</example>

<example>
<new_task>remove the deprecated session login flow</new_task>
<existing_tasks>
#7 (executing): add session-backed login flow to AuthRepository
</existing_tasks>
{"duplicate": false, "duplicate_of": 0, "reason": "opposite operation — one adds a flow, the other removes it"}
</example>
